H={'christmasstart_dyk': "When does Christmas season really start? Mariah Carey says it's time, but every country has its own clock. America: right after Thanksgiving, when the tree goes up and Black Friday begins. Australia: early December, with carols and barbecues in the summer heat. The UK: mid November, when the famous Christmas lights switch on in London. Germany: late November, when the Christmas markets open for Advent. The Netherlands: only after Sinterklaas leaves on December fifth! But here's the twist. The Philippines: Christmas starts on September first, in the ber months, and lasts until January, the longest Christmas season on Earth! When do you put up your tree? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'holidaytravel_dyk': "What if holiday traffic was nine billion trips? Let's compare the biggest trips home. Australia: Christmas is summer break, so airports and highways are packed for weeks. The UK: the days before Christmas bring millions of car journeys. America: about eighty million people travel for Thanksgiving! Indonesia: about one hundred ninety million people go home for Mudik, at the end of Ramadan. India: the Kumbh Mela festival counted over six hundred million visits in twenty twenty five! But here's the twist. China: the Lunar New Year rush hit about nine billion trips in just forty days, the biggest human migration on Earth! How far do you travel for the holidays? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'hippos_dyk': "What if wild hippos lived in your country? Let's check the wild giants around the world. America: Florida has about one point three million alligators! Australia: about one hundred thousand saltwater crocodiles in the Northern Territory alone. India: about three thousand seven hundred wild tigers, three quarters of all wild tigers on Earth. Indonesia: about three thousand Komodo dragons, the biggest lizards alive. South Africa: Kruger Park alone has over three thousand hippos. But here's the twist. Colombia: about two hundred wild hippos, all from just four that drug lord Pablo Escobar brought to his private zoo! Which wild animal would scare you most? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'datacenters_dyk': "What if AI data centers took over your country's power? Let's count the data centers. Australia: about three hundred, mostly around Sydney and Melbourne. Canada: about three hundred thirty, helped by cheap hydro power. The UK: about five hundred, many around London. Germany: about five hundred twenty, with Frankfurt as a huge hub. America: over five thousand, more than the next ten countries combined, and northern Virginia is the data center capital of the world! But here's the twist. Ireland: data centers use about twenty two percent of the whole country's electricity, up from just five percent ten years earlier! Should AI get its own power plants? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'rain_dyk': "What if it rained almost every day where you live? After record rain in California, let's compare yearly rainfall. Australia: about five hundred thirty millimeters, the driest inhabited continent. America: about seven hundred fifteen millimeters on average. The UK: about one thousand two hundred, famous for its drizzle! Japan: about one thousand seven hundred, with a whole rainy season in June. Colombia: about three thousand two hundred, one of the wettest countries on Earth! But here's the twist. India: the village of Mawsynram gets about eleven thousand eight hundred millimeters a year, the rainiest place on Earth! Do you love the rain? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'election_dyk': "Why is election day on a Tuesday? Let's compare election days. America: the Tuesday after the first Monday in November, a rule from eighteen forty five, so farmers could travel by horse and buggy! The UK: always on a Thursday, by tradition. Canada: usually a Monday in October. Germany: always a Sunday, so most people are off work. Australia: always a Saturday, voting is compulsory, and you can grab a democracy sausage at the polling station! But here's the twist. India: elections take about six weeks, with almost a billion voters, and there's even a polling station in a lion forest for just one voter! What day should elections be? Comment below, and subscribe for more!", 'capitals_dyk': "What if your biggest city wasn't the capital? It happens a lot. America: the capital is Washington DC, not New York, which has more than ten times as many people. Australia: Canberra, not Sydney, built as a compromise between Sydney and Melbourne! Canada: Ottawa, not Toronto, picked by Queen Victoria. Brazil: Brasília, not São Paulo, a whole new city built in about four years. Turkey: Ankara, not Istanbul, even though Istanbul is far more famous. But here's the twist. South Africa: it has three capitals, Pretoria for the government, Cape Town for parliament, and Bloemfontein for the top appeals court! Did you know your capital? Comment below, and subscribe for more!"}
